*💻 JavaScript Roadmap 2026 — Part 7*

*🟢 Objects in JavaScript*

Arrays are useful when you have a collection of values.

But what if you want to represent something with properties and related information?

For example, a customer has:

- Name
- Age
- Email
- City

An object is perfect for this.

*1️⃣ Creating an Object*

An object is created using curly braces "{}".
const person = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25,
    city: "Pune"
};

Here:

name → "Rahul"
age → 25
city → "Pune"

These are called properties.

Think of an object as a collection of related information.

*2️⃣ Accessing Properties*

There are two common ways.

Dot notation
console.log(person.name);
Output:
Rahul
Bracket notation
console.log(person["age"]);

Output:
25

Both access the same object.

*3️⃣ Dot Notation vs Bracket Notation*

Dot notation:
person.name
is convenient when you know the property name.

Bracket notation becomes especially useful when the property name is stored in a variable.
const property = "name";

console.log(person[property]);

Output:
Rahul

This would not work the same way:
person.property
because JavaScript would look for a property literally named ""property"".

*4️⃣ Adding Properties*

You can add a new property to an existing object.

const person = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25
};

person.city = "Pune";

console.log(person);
Now the object contains:

name → Rahul
age → 25
city → Pune

*5️⃣ Updating Properties*

You can change an existing property.
person.age = 26;

console.log(person.age);

Output:
26

This is possible even when the object itself was declared using "const".

Remember:
const person = {};
prevents reassignment of "person", but the object's properties can still be modified.

*6️⃣ Deleting Properties*

Use the "delete" operator.
delete person.city;
Now "city" no longer exists on the object.

Use this carefully in real applications because changing object structure unnecessarily can have performance and maintainability implications.

*🔹 Objects Can Store Different Data Types*

*7️⃣ Strings, Numbers and Booleans*
const user = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25,
    isActive: true
};

Objects can also contain arrays:
const user = {
    name: "Rahul",
    skills: ["SQL", "Python", "JavaScript"]
};

And even other objects:
const user = {
    name: "Rahul",
    address: {
        city: "Pune",
        country: "India"
    }
};

This is called a nested object.

*8️⃣ Accessing Nested Objects*

Given:
const user = {
    name: "Rahul",
    address: {
        city: "Pune",
        country: "India"
    }
};

You can access the city using:
console.log(user.address.city);

Output:
Pune

Or:
console.log(user["address"]["city"]);

*🔹 Object Methods*

*9️⃣ Functions Inside Objects*

A function stored inside an object is commonly called a method.

const person = {
    name: "Rahul",

    greet() {
        console.log("Hello!");
    }
};

Call it using:
person.greet();

Output:
Hello!

Methods are useful when an object contains both data and behavior.

*🔟 Using "this"*

Inside an object method, "this" can refer to the object that the method is called on.

const person = {
    name: "Rahul",

    greet() {
        console.log("Hello " + this.name);
    }
};

person.greet();

Output:
Hello Rahul

Here:
this.name
refers to the "name" property of the object used to call the method.

Understanding "this" deeply will become important later.

*🔹 Object Shorthand*

*1️⃣1️⃣ Property Shorthand*

Suppose you already have variables:
const name = "Rahul";
const age = 25;
Instead of:
const person = {
    name: name,
    age: age
};

Modern JavaScript lets you write:
const person = {
    name,
    age
};

JavaScript understands that:
name: name
age: age
can be shortened to:
name
age

*🔹 Computed Properties*

*1️⃣2️⃣ Dynamic Property Names*

You can use an expression inside square brackets when creating an object.
const property = "name";

const person = {
    [property]: "Rahul"
};

console.log(person.name);

Output:
Rahul

This becomes useful when object property names are determined dynamically.

*🔹 Object Destructuring*

*1️⃣3️⃣ What Is Destructuring?*

Destructuring allows you to extract values from an object into variables.

Given:
const person = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25,
    city: "Pune"
};

You can write:
const { name, age } = person;

console.log(name);
console.log(age);

Output:
Rahul
25

Instead of repeatedly writing:
person.name
person.age
you now have variables:
name
age

*1️⃣4️⃣ Renaming During Destructuring*

You can give the extracted value a different variable name.

const person = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25
};

const { name: userName } = person;

console.log(userName);

Output:
Rahul

The syntax:
name: userName
means:

Take the name property → store it in userName

*1️⃣5️⃣ Default Values in Destructuring*

If a property doesn't exist, you can provide a default.
const person = {
    name: "Rahul"
};

const { name, city = "Pune" } = person;

console.log(city);

Output:
Pune

*🔹 Spread Syntax*

*1️⃣6️⃣ Copying Object Properties*

The spread operator "..." can copy properties into another object.
const person = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25
};

const updatedPerson = {
   ...person,
    city: "Pune"
};

Now:
console.log(updatedPerson);
contains:

name → Rahul
age → 25
city → Pune

This pattern is very common in modern JavaScript applications.

*1️⃣7️⃣ Updating an Object Using Spread*

Suppose:
const user = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25
};

You want a new object with a different age.
const updatedUser = {
   ...user,
    age: 26
};

Now:
user.age → 25
updatedUser.age → 26
The original object hasn't been modified.

This is an important pattern when working with immutable state.

*🔹 Object Utility Methods*

*1️⃣8️⃣ "Object.keys()"*

Returns an array containing an object's property names.

const person = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25,
    city: "Pune"
};

console.log(Object.keys(person));
Output:
["name", "age", "city"]

*1️⃣9️⃣ "Object.values()"*

Returns an array containing the values.
console.log(Object.values(person));
Output:
["Rahul", 25, "Pune"]

*2️⃣0️⃣ "Object.entries()"*

Returns key-value pairs.
console.log(Object.entries(person));
Output:
[
    ["name", "Rahul"],
    ["age", 25],
    ["city", "Pune"]
]

This is particularly useful when you need to iterate through an object's properties.

*🔄 Iterating Through Objects*

*2️⃣1️⃣ Using "for...in"*
const person = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 25,
    city: "Pune"
};

for (const key in person) {
    console.log(key, person[key]);
}

Output:
name Rahul
age 25
city Pune

*2️⃣2️⃣ Using "Object.entries()"*

Another modern approach:
for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(person)) {
    console.log(key, value);
}

This uses array destructuring to separate each key-value pair.

*⭐ Objects and Arrays Together*

Real applications rarely use objects and arrays separately.

Usually, you'll work with an array of objects.

For example:
const employees = [
    {
        name: "Amit",
        salary: 50000
    },
    {
        name: "Priya",
        salary: 70000
    },
    {
        name: "Rahul",
        salary: 45000
    }
];

Now you can combine what you learned in Part 6.

*Get employee names*
const names = employees.map(employee => employee.name);

console.log(names);

Output:
["Amit", "Priya", "Rahul"]


*Find high earners*
const highEarners = employees.filter(
    employee => employee.salary > 50000
);

*Find one employee*
const employee = employees.find(
    employee => employee.name === "Priya"
);

This combination of arrays + objects is fundamental to working with API responses and application data.

*⭐ Optional Chaining with Objects*

Suppose an API gives you:
const user = {
    profile: {
        address: {
            city: "Pune"
        }
    }
};

You can access:
console.log(user.profile.address.city);
But what if "address" doesn't exist?

Optional chaining helps:
console.log(user.profile?.address?.city);

If the property doesn't exist, JavaScript returns:
undefined
instead of throwing an error at that point in the chain.

This is extremely useful when working with external API data.

*⭐ Object References*

This is an important concept.

Consider:
const user1 = {
    name: "Rahul"
};

const user2 = user1;

user2.name = "Amit";

console.log(user1.name);

What do you think it prints?
Amit

Why?

"user2" doesn't contain an independent copy of the object.

Both variables refer to the same object.

Conceptually:
user1 ─────┐
           ↓
        { name: "Amit" }
           ↑
user2 ─────┘

Understanding object references is essential before learning advanced JavaScript.

*🧠 Real-World Example*

Imagine an application storing customer information:
const customer = {
    id: 101,
    name: "Amit",
    email: "amit@example.com",

    address: {
        city: "Pune",
        country: "India"
    },

    orders: [
        {
            product: "Laptop",
            amount: 60000
        },
        {
            product: "Mouse",
            amount: 1000
        }
    ]
};

You can access:
customer.name

customer.address.city

customer.orders[0].product

This structure resembles the nested data you commonly encounter in real applications and APIs.

*⚠️ Common Beginner Mistakes*

Mistake 1: Confusing dot and bracket notation

Correct:
person.name
Correct:
person["name"]
But if the property name is stored in a variable:
const key = "name";

person[key]
is what you want.

Mistake 2: Assuming "const" makes objects immutable

This is valid:
const user = {
    name: "Rahul"
};

user.name = "Amit";
"const" prevents reassignment of the variable, not modification of the object's properties.

Mistake 3: Confusing copying with referencing

This:
const user2 = user1;
doesn't create an independent object.

For a shallow copy:
const user2 = {...user1 };
But remember: spread only performs a shallow copy.

Nested objects can still share references.

*💻 Practice*

*1️⃣ Create an Object*

Create a "student" object containing:

name
age
course
city

Print each property.

*2️⃣ Modify a Property*

Change the student's age.

*3️⃣ Add a Property*

Add:

email

to the object.

*4️⃣ Destructuring*

Extract "name" and "course" using object destructuring.

*5️⃣ Nested Object*

Create:
const employee = {
    name: "...",
    department: {
        name: "...",
        location: "..."
    }
};

Print the department location.

*6️⃣ Array of Objects*

Create an array of five employees.

Use "filter()" to find employees whose salary is greater than "60000".

*7️⃣ Challenge*

Given:
const products = [
    { name: "Laptop", price: 60000 },
    { name: "Phone", price: 30000 },
    { name: "Tablet", price: 20000 },
    { name: "Monitor", price: 15000 }
];

Try to:

1. Get all product names.
2. Find products costing more than 20000.
3. Find the first product costing less than 18000.
4. Calculate the total price of all products.
5. Create a new array where every product has a 10% discount.

